# Git workflow

Authoritative detail lives in `docs/engineering/git-workflow.md`. Read it before answering anything it covers.
This skill is the fast path.

## Model
GitHub Flow. `main` is production and protected. Short-lived branches (1–3 days) off `main`, one per ticket,
PR with a Vercel preview, 1 approval (2 for `core/`, `shared/`, auth, payments), squash merge, delete branch.
No `develop` branch.

## Starting work
```bash
git checkout main && git pull
git checkout -b feat/MIL-231-seat-allocation
```
Branch prefixes: `feat/ fix/ chore/ refactor/ docs/ hotfix/`.

## Commits
`type(scope): imperative subject`, lowercase, no trailing period. Body explains *why*. Footer carries the ticket.
Never propose `--no-verify`. If husky/lint-staged rejects a commit, fix the code.

## Raising a PR
Fill the template in `.github/pull_request_template.md`: what, why (ticket), how to test, screenshots for any
UI change (mobile 375px + desktop), and the checklist. Before review and again before merge:
```bash
git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main && git push --force-with-lease
```
`--force-with-lease`, never plain `--force`, and never on a shared branch.

## Reviewing a diff
Check: scope matches the ticket; PR under ~400 lines; no new suppressions; structure and import boundaries
from `AGENTS.md`; Tailwind instead of new CSS; tests for the changed behavior; screenshots present for UI.

## Merge conflicts after the structure refactor
Paths moved en masse, so for a branch that predates the refactor use **merge, not rebase**:
```bash
git checkout main && git pull
git checkout your-branch
git merge main
```
"Deleted by them" means the file moved. Find the new path with
`git log --diff-filter=R --find-renames -- old/path`, apply your change there, and `git add` the new path.
Rebasing here makes you resolve the same conflict once per commit.

## Never
Commit to `main` directly · force-push a shared branch · commit `.env`, keys, `dist/`, `node_modules/`,
`.angular/`, `.DS_Store` · hand-edit `pnpm-lock.yaml` · leave a branch open for weeks.